Vendor runtime images are referenced by immutable registry digest and are not
claimed as NPA-published images. NVIDIA's AnomalyGen image fails SkyPilot
bootstrap, so the checked-in restricted `paidf-anomalygen-sky` image builds the
exact Apache-2.0 source revision on digest-pinned public CUDA bases. It belongs
in an operator-controlled registry and may never be published to NPA's public
GHCR namespace without a separate redistribution review. NPA runtime-fetches exact
Apache-2.0 protocol source where the selected vendor service image does not
carry its client, and verifies the fetched commit before execution. All
registered components use S3-compatible object storage for handoffs.
Each run writes `reports/upstream.json` (`npa.paidf.upstream.v1`) so this
adaptation boundary and the exact public revisions travel with its artifacts.

IAA and EVG generation also require restricted operator-built wrappers:
paidf-image-edit-sky and paidf-event-video-sky use exact upstream
vLLM-Omni parent digests listed in the container catalog and add SkyPilot
worker prerequisites, an isolated NPA environment, and argument forwarding. IAA
uses the aligned upstream 0.22.0 release to fix CVE-2026-48746 in the blueprint's
0.20 runtime, preserving the Qwen model revision and image-edit protocol.
The original blueprint digest remains separately attributed in upstream.json.
Reference: https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/security/advisories/GHSA-94f4-hr76-p5j6
Actual upstream
bootstrap probes failed before generation. These source recipes do not claim
built-byte security clearance or publication; the complete inherited dependency
closure must be reviewed before any change to private-only redistribution.

Runtime model snapshots are also pinned and remain outside published images:

  Qwen/Qwen-Image-Edit-2511
  revision 6f3ccc0b56e431dc6a0c2b2039706d7d26f22cb9

  nvidia/Cosmos3-Super-Image2Video
  revision 4f847566f3d3388fbf0ac07b99dd1a6432db9ecd

EVG detection downloads the public RF-DETR Base checkpoint at runtime. The
pinned auto-labeling source requires SHA-256
d8f70210e425a4a4234d547737f57500bcc4ac24a333b99e33d9d5a371e0b80f;
NPA preserves this verification even when an operator supplies a custom cache
filename. The selected BoostTrack adapter does not load a ReID embedder.

The DIG runtime additionally consumes the exact public snapshots pinned by
NVIDIA/paidf-anomalygen: Cosmos3 Nano and Edge, DINOv2 Large, C-RADIOv3-B,
Wan2.2 TI2V-5B's VAE, SAM2.1 Hiera Large, Qwen3Guard 0.6B, and the Qwen3-VL 8B
tokenizer. Cosmos Guardrail1 remains a separately gated runtime fetch. The NPA
model-access catalog records every public revision/probe and the exact gated
payload; none is embedded in the restricted compatibility image.

IAA/EVG labeling requires four restricted compatibility recipes:
paidf-detection-sky, paidf-captioning-sky, paidf-visual-qa-sky, and
paidf-attribute-search-sky. Each retains its exact vendor-owned NGC parent
digest, upstream user and /app/.venv service environment. It adds missing
SkyPilot prerequisites and Git for the pinned orchestration-asset checkout,
then replaces ENTRYPOINT ["main"] with argument forwarding. The original
service CLI remains /app/.venv/bin/main. NPA setup installs into a separate
owner-writable /opt/npa-venv, with PATH preserved for login shells; it does not
install dependencies into the vendor /app/.venv. These recipes add no model weights,
datasets, credentials or recorded consent. The complete inherited image layers
still require payload/license/security review; neither source inspection nor
private registry access establishes permission to redistribute gated weights.
They are excluded from NPA public publication and are not claimed built or
functionally accepted merely because their source recipes exist.

NPA's toolRef access closure probes each exact NGC parent repository under the
operator's credential before submission. That proves pull entitlement only and
does not grant redistribution rights. Runtime pullability and bootstrap must be
proved separately against each operator-built wrapper's exact private digest.

Gated weights remain runtime fetches under the operator's own upstream identity.
Access credentials prove fetch authorization, not permission to redistribute
weights. See `skills/NOTICE-NVIDIA-COSMOS-OSS` and
`skills/NOTICE-NVIDIA-COSMOS3` for the executable component boundaries.

